UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The @C3 lead worked out well for us, but I would have led a spade. Probably doesn't matter, but my preference, as the declarer, would have been to ruff the spade, and draw trump, and, assuming they are 2-1, and claim. So if trumps are 2-1 West always has 10 tricks--8 hearts and 2 black aces. |
PG| ***** 12 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|A spade lead turns out to help declarer. He can win the @SA, take the ruffing finesse in spades, draw trump and win a 2nd spade trick and make 5. |
PG| ***** 13 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If North were a bit stronger he could make a ^*BCard Showing^*n double saying it was our hand and I should Do Something Intelligent^*n. |
PG| ***** 14 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Doubles of preempts of 3@S and above are never pure penalty. They are just value showing and normally do not show trump. If you think about it, if the opponents preempt, it is very unlikely you have trump length but it is often the case you have values. |
PG| ***** 15 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|So the best you can do when that is the case is double. Often the double will be passed but if opener has an unbalanced hand and is short in the preemptive suit, he might pull. Admittedly it is all a lot of guess work, but that's why preempts are so effective.|
PG| ***** 16 ***** |


