nt|^z@3^b@2^^|lb|*yShould South bid?^^n|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I hate to pass. I consider 1N. But it turns out that I play 1N overcalls when not vulnerable are 14-17 rather than 15-18. However, here I am at unfavorable vulnerability (our side vulnerable, their side not). So I need to be more cautious especially since I have clubs and that was opener's suit.|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


mb|P|mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|sk|w|lb|*bWhat should West bid here?^^1H|mb|1H|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With 4 hearts and 4 spades West must bid 1@H. 1@S denies 4 hearts. If West bids 1@S and then rebids hearts later, that shows 5+ spades and 4+ hearts.|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


mb|P|mb|2H|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|3N|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Many players have a tendency to continue to describe their hand when there is no need to. On this hand, West knows they should be in 4@H so they just should bid it. 2@H limits the East hand to 15 or 16 or so, so slam is not possible.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^*BWest: ^*nHow do you know? Couldn't 3N be better.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|You have a heart fit, although admittedly East could have 3 card support. But if East has 4 hearts you want to be in 4@H not 3N.|
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BWest: ^*n3N gives Partner the choices of pass or on to 4@H.|
PG| ***** 26 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That's true. If West wanted to bid 3N that actually might be slightly better in case partner only had 3@H. But in this case with nice 4 card support and a small doubleton spade, 4@H is clearly best.|
PG| ***** 27 ***** |


mb|P|mb|4H|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 28 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|CJ|pc|C2|pc|CK|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|West should be very suspicious of the @CJ given the @C10 is in dummy. In fact playing low is going to cause him to go down because East opened 1@C and my partner led the suit. Normally partner would have led either a trump or an unbid suit. I am pretty sure partner had a singleton club.|
PG| ***** 29 ***** |


pc|C4|
PG| ***** 30 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Based on what I said in the last page, I am going to lead a club for partner to ruff. And when I lead a suit back for partner to ruff the size of my spot is suit preference. If I lead a high club back (e.g. the @C9 here) that asks partner to lead back the higher of the remaining suits (spades here).|
PG| ***** 31 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If I lead a low club back, that asks partner to lead back the lower of the remaining suits (diamonds here). On this hand since I have aces in both side suits it doesn't matter what partner leads back. But I am going to lead a low club back because a diamond lead might be slightly more advantageous. |
PG| ***** 32 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|sk||cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|When partner leads back the suit, he should lead back attitude meaning he should lead a low diamond if he has an honor, and a high diamond if no honor. And if he leads back a low diamond I can play the @DJ knowing partner's honor must be the @DQ. Of course, here he leads back a high diamond.|
PG| ***** 33 ***** |


