UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It turns out even if declarer plays the @CA at trick 1 (which is the correct play) the hand will go down because, although double dummy West has no heart losers since the @CJ is likely a singleton, that means it is more likely North has longer trump than South (which in fact was the case).|
PG| ***** 43 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|There are two important takeaways from this hand:

1) when you lead back a suit for partner to ruff, the size of the spot you lead back is suit preference for which suit you want partner to lead back after he ruffs;

2) be aware of trump promotion positions, such as this hand.|
PG| ***** 44 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Finally, I guess 3N turns out to work out better on this hand because North has no entries. 
|
PG| ***** 45 ***** |


